WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.100
- Many industries are struggling
with a worker shortage.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.420
- Businesses large and
small are all on the hunt

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.600
for workers, from mega
retailers to mom and pop shops.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.120
- [Narrator] As companies
begin staffing up

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.420
for the holiday season,

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.160
many say a nationwide labor shortage

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.778
is increasingly a challenge
for their businesses.

00:00:16.778 --> 00:00:20.170
- It's probably the tightest
labor market we've ever seen.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:22.300
- There are sort of plenty
of seasonal positions,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.710
way more openings right now

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.470
than there are job seekers.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.910
- To lure workers away
from the competition

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.430
in a year of mass resignations

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.500
and stepped up labor organizing,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.163
companies are sweetening
their hiring packages.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.490
This is the Employment Cost Index,

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.490
a metric that measures
worker compensation.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.470
This summer, it rose
1.3% in the third quarter

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.470
from the second,

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.090
the fastest jump since at least 2001.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.500
Costco raised its minimum
US wage to $17 an hour.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.460
Amazon, which is looking to
hire 150,000 seasonal staff

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.960
is offering bonuses of up to $3,000

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.740
in certain areas.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.050
Companies are also getting creative

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.240
with incentives, especially when it comes

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.980
to the application process.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.160
- Many companies, for years,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.080
have had sort of in-depth applications

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.470
where it might have asked for
extended employment history

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.010
or educational background.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:13.960
Many companies have said

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.560
that they've looked at their applications

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.960
and stripped out anything
that isn't absolutely relevant

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.470
either for government requirements

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.620
or their own background
check requirements.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.788
- [Narrator] To encourage
more applicants to apply,

00:01:25.788 --> 00:01:28.460
the cosmetics, skincare
and perfume company,

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.450
The Body Shop,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.860
is eliminating not just
education requirements

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.895
but also drug tests, background checks

00:01:33.895 --> 00:01:38.070
and expectations for a
minimum level of experience.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.880
Southwest Airlines, which
recently had to cut flights

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.690
because of its staffing shortage

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.930
is now making on-the-spot job offers

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.100
after long shunning the practice.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.090
Last month the company
reverts its earlier position

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.600
requiring vaccinations for employees

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.800
after aviation unions pushed
back against the rule,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.950
raising the possibility of
holiday service problems.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.810
Southwest says it now
plans to allow employees

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.390
with approved medical or religious reasons

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.223
for avoiding vaccinations
to continue working.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.300
One of the biggest companies
staffing up this holiday season

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.900
is the United Parcel Service, or UPS,

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.520
which said it's looking to
hire more than 100,000 people.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.490
- Record-breaking levels of packages

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:21.740
are expected this year.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.530
- Shipping companies like FedEx and UPS

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.670
are frantically searching
for help right now.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:28.900
- We are looking to hire drivers,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.350
both tractor trailer drivers

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.193
and local delivery drivers.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.810
We're also looking to hire
part-time package handlers,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.970
and that would be the
largest number of people

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.427
we're looking for.

00:02:40.427 --> 00:02:43.300
(upbeat music)

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.254
- [Narrator] To help attract
those seasonal workers,

00:02:45.254 --> 00:02:47.967
UPS streamlined its application process

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.640
so the company now responds to applicants

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.420
with offers within 30 minutes

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.610
of submitting the application.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.500
In the past, applicants
wouldn't hear back from UPS

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.870
for about two weeks.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.530
- It's been extremely successful.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.850
In fact, they survey all of our applicants

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.883
and they ask 'em how the process was,

00:03:05.883 --> 00:03:08.140
and the numbers went through the roof

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.840
in terms of positive feelings

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.100
about that whole process.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.970
They pivoted towards a digital approach

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:14.950
to hiring people.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.840
70% or more of all of our hires

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.630
are done on a cell phone,

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.540
and we were nowhere near
that three years ago.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.050
- [Narrator] UPS has also
revamped its outreach strategy

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:27.410
to potential employees.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.210
- We're trying to find
people where they live.

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.210
We're trying to find people
where they spend their time.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.710
Well, these days, that's on social media.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.480
Between 70 and 75%

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.590
of our advertising spend today

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:41.640
is done on those social channels

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.850
versus about five years ago

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:49.350
when 70% to 75% was done
on traditional channels

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:52.460
like print and broadcast advertising.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.300
So it's a complete flip flop if you will

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.780
of where we're spending our money

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.230
and how we're trying to reach people.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.230
- [Narrator] After the financial
crisis of 2007 and 2008

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:05.970
led to a rapid increase in unemployment,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.640
many employers had their pick

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.410
of qualified job candidates.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:10.890
- So after the Great Recession,

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.740
many employers inflated the
requirements needed for jobs,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.890
asking, for example,
for bachelor's degrees

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.700
for IT help desk desk specialists,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.330
or construction supervisors.

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:21.830
Jobs that were historically held

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:23.550
by high school graduates.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.040
This left many non-degree holders

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.110
with few options besides
low-paid service jobs

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.853
that didn't offer as much
career or wage growth.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.100
- [Narrator] But today
this decade plus period

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.840
of qualification inflation

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.340
is beginning to reverse

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.430
as employers emphasize
speed over credentials

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:44.710
to adapt to a historically
tight job market.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.170
Last year 1.5 million jobs

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:49.400
were closed to people
without a college degree

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.590
but since then, the
percentage of employers

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.870
that require a college degree dropped

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.190
from just over 20% in January 2019

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:00.060
to about 15% this October.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.590
- Many workforce specialists

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.880
and employers have said
that those requirements

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:05.570
often weren't necessary.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.210
Some companies have looked, for example,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.330
at whether asking about GPA has any impact

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:11.290
on their performance.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:13.100
And many have found that it doesn't.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.270
- [Narrator] Economists
say that these changes

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.670
could also narrow the gap

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:19.000
between the 2.5 unemployment rate

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.110
of college graduates and the 5.8 rate

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.520
of job seekers with a high school diploma.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.670
- So right now there's a change afoot.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.510
There's a movement towards
more skill-based hiring,

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.080
looking at potential rather than degrees.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.110
And many economists say

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.190
this could outlast the
current hiring emergency.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:37.900
Many think we'll see more of a movement

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:39.260
towards looking at skills

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.862
rather than someone's past
experiences or degrees.

00:05:41.862 --> 00:05:44.529
(playful music)

